  <!-- what a theme costs -->
  <data name="ThemeCautionCathode" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Six brightnesses of one phosphor. No two of them are far enough apart to be told apart, so on this theme a control is known by where it sits and what it is called, never by its color.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionAmberConsole" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A lamp in a dark room. The ramp runs like heat, so it still works without color vision, but the deepest ember is light rather than letters: give it a rim, a value or a fill, not a label.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionTerminalGreen" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A lamp in a dark room. Every color here survives being filled and labeled except the deepest green, which is too dark to carry type on a lit control.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionBigwig" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Red is for light, not for words. It is bright enough for a knob's ring, a fader's fill or a lit button, but too dark to read as a name, so give record and panic their names in the ink.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionJove" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The tabs are their color, at full strength, all the time. That reads beautifully on a panel of a few dozen switches and turns into a wall of color on a page of a few hundred, so keep the colored switches to the ones that matter.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionSupersaw" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A switch that is on looks exactly like a switch that is off, apart from its lamp. That is what makes a rack of them readable at arm's length, but it does mean the state is one small light rather than a whole button.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionFiveIsh" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Color is only a lamp here. Knobs and faders are silver, black and white, so you can't group them by color. Put controls on the green or on the black, not on the tan: a white value on the tan is too faint to read.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionAirySystem" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A light show, on purpose. Everything that turns or slides glows all the time, so on a busy page a touch has less to stand out against. It's made for a dark room.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionTonal" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Every control carries a wash of its own color at rest, so a busy page is colorful even when nothing is happening and activity has less room to stand out.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionHighContrast" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Hard edges, no glow and no rounding. It is offered, never forced: if Windows switches to high contrast during a set, the running layout is left alone and the offer waits until the layout is next opened.</value>
  </data>

