  <data name="ThemeCautionOffworld" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A busy wall, made for a dark room. Rain and stains are part of the page, so keep names on the sections rather than on the bare wall. The flares are only decoration: a light coming on is still what shows the state.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionGroovy" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A bright page, made for a lit room. The six colors are close in brightness, so they're told apart by color alone: name your controls and group them by where they sit.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionGroovyDark" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Made for a dark room. A switch that is on is a solid block of color, so keep colored switches to the ones that matter. The six colors are close in brightness, and with red-green color blindness red and avocado look alike.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionSoftSector" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A light theme, so it's bright on a dark stage. Red, amber and green are the three colors hardest to tell apart with red-green color blindness. A switch that's on also stays pressed down, so its state isn't shown by color alone.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionHardSector" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Red, amber and green are the three colors hardest to tell apart with red-green color blindness. A switch that's on also stays pressed down, so its state isn't shown by color alone.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionInsertCoin" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Every button shows its color all the time, which suits a page of pads more than a page of two hundred switches. Round buttons want square controls. With red-green color blindness, blue and purple look alike, so don't use them for opposite jobs.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionChicago" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Mostly gray, with the color in small places: title bars, tags and values. It's not made for a dark stage, and the teal background is loud. With red-green color blindness, green and olive look alike, so don't use them for opposite jobs.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionNightDrive" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A picture is a lot to put behind a control surface. The sun pulls your eye to the middle of the page, so a layout with its controls there hides the best part. Made for a dark room.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionVisor" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Thin lines want a close screen or a big one: at arm's length on a small tablet, the corners are hard to find. Nothing is filled until it's on, which is quick once you know the page and slower for somebody new to it. Made for a dark room.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ThemeCautionGoodForm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A light theme, so it's bright on a dark stage. A key at rest is the color of the panel, and only its shadow sets it apart. Pads have no color until they're on. With red-green color blindness, orange and mustard look alike, so don't use them for opposite jobs.</value>
  </data>
